<http://data.silknow.org/object/83eb64dc-0ef5-3f92-a4ed-0f5366dbef62/observation/4>
        a                   crmsci:S4_Observation ;
        ecrm:P2_has_type    <http://data.silknow.org/observation/general-observation> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note    "Shoes (Pianelle) \nLate 16th century\n\nOriginally a form of backless overshoe, pianelle were sometimes worn alone. No distinction was made between the left and the right foot in the shape of the shoe. Although these shoes have a small platform, they are low compared to the extremely high ones fashionable in Venice at the time. Women sometimes included shoes in their trousseaus. [57 words]\n\nItaly, probably Venice\nWood, covered with velvet, with gilded and stamped leather inner sole, and gilded silver braid and bobbin lace; holes for lacing\n\nV&A: 929&A-1901 [5 Oct 2006 - 7 Jan 2007]"@en ;
        crmsci:O8_observed  <http://data.silknow.org/object/83eb64dc-0ef5-3f92-a4ed-0f5366dbef62> .

